WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.880
A sidecar places defined power functions beside the I T rack rather

2
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.600
than assuming they all sit inside it.

3
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.420
That can change how much equipment space is available and where conversion

4
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.620
or buffering is located.

5
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.560
The Diablo four hundred Academy course provides a detailed example of a

6
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:15.780
power-sidecar design.

7
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.840
Relocation creates integration obligations.

8
00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.860
The connection between power equipment and I T equipment must support the

9
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.040
intended electrical and mechanical conditions.

10
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:30.160
The combined footprint, cooling, access and handling requirements must fit the site.

11
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.280
A design that frees space inside one frame may consume space or

12
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.440
service clearance elsewhere.

13
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.780
Compare the complete arrangement against the deployment envelope.

14
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.260
Identify which functions moved, which stayed, and who owns the interconnect and

15
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.080
protection boundary.

16
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.540
Don't infer that every sidecar has the same input voltage, output voltage

17
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:49.380
or capacity.

18
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.560
The useful choice is a supported architecture that addresses a real constraint,

19
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.480
not a sidecar label attached to an otherwise incomplete rack proposal.

